+# Crash points: what is on the device when a client has been told "stored"
+
+`Spool::append` answers a client once the record is written and readable. This page is the rest of that
+sentence: it lists every way the store can stop, and says for each one what is on the device, what
+recovery will see, which policy loses what, and what the operator ends up explaining. The decision
+these points belong to is [docs/adr/0001-write-path.md](adr/0001-write-path.md), which names the policy
+this build ships; the policies themselves are [`Policy`](../src/engine/wal/policy.rs) and the call that
+bounds every window below is [`Spool::sync`](../src/engine/spool.rs).
+
+Two words are used precisely, and most arguments about this are really arguments about them:
+
+- **written** — the bytes are in the operating system's page cache, any reader on this machine sees
+  them, and a process death does not take them;
+- **durable** — a flush this store asked for was answered by the device.
+
+| # | the crash | which policies lose what |
+| - | --------- | ------------------------ |
+| 1 | the process dies | |
+| 2 | the machine dies | |
+| 3 | the machine dies mid-write | |
+| 4 | the directory entry was never flushed | |
+| 5 | the flush itself fails | |
+| 6 | the process stops cleanly | |
+
+### 1 — The process dies after the write and before any sync
+
+<What is on the device, what recovery sees, which policies lose what here, and what the operator
+observes. The first two points on this page differ only in *which* thing died, and the difference is
+the whole point of the page.>
+
+### 2 — The machine dies after the write and before any sync
+
+<Same four questions. Name the policies and the bound each one puts on the loss.>
+
+### 3 — The machine dies during a write (a torn record)
+
+<Same four questions. Say what the last record looks like on the device, and what a reader can and
+cannot tell about it without a checksum.>
+
+### 4 — The file's data is durable but its directory entry is not
+
+<Same four questions. Say which write makes a store *exist* for a reader, and which one does not.>
+
+### 5 — The flush itself fails (the device, not the store)
+
+<Same four questions. Say what "acknowledged" turns into when the device answers a flush with an error,
+and what the store has to stop claiming.>
+
+### 6 — The process stops cleanly (SIGTERM, and what "clean" is worth)
+
+<Same four questions. A clean stop is not a crash — say what it does and does not put on the device,
+and which window survives into the reboot that follows it.>
