+//! When the write path waits for the device — as a value, because a decision nobody can point at is
+//! a decision nobody keeps.
+//!
+//! Step `lakelet/4.3-write-path-adr`. An ingest accepts a record and answers the client that sent
+//! it; between those two facts sits a choice with a number attached: how long the store may hold an
+//! acknowledged record in the operating system's page cache before it is on the device. This module
+//! is that choice as code — [`Policy`], the three answers, and [`Losses`], what each of them gives up
+//! when the *machine* stops. (A process that dies loses none of it: the kernel still holds the bytes
+//! and the file is intact. Every word in this module about losing is about the host, or the device,
+//! which is the case that makes the policy a decision.)
+//!
+//! Two properties are why this is a module and not a match arm somewhere:
+//!
+//! * **the decision is a value** — [`Policy::DEFAULT`] is what this build does, [`Policy::ALL`] is
+//!   what it can be asked for, and [`Policy::parse`] is how a document spells one;
+//! * **the decision is checkable against its own argument** — every policy answers what it loses, and
+//!   `docs/crash-points.md` has to name every policy in `ALL`. Drift between the table an operator
+//!   reads and the value the build ships is the failure this shape exists to prevent.
+//!
+//! The argument itself — what one durable write costs on the machine this was measured on, why the
+//! default interval is the number it is, and what each policy accepts losing — is
+//! `docs/adr/0001-write-path.md`. If that page and this module disagree, the module wins and the page
+//! is a bug; the step's spec is what holds the two together.
+//!
+//! Every body below is this step's work. The doc comments are the contract the step's spec and
+//! documents hold the work to, including the two constants: what this build *does* and what it can be
+//! asked for are decisions, not defaults somebody else already made.
+
+use crate::error::Error;
+
+/// When the write path waits for the device.
+///
+/// Every answer here is a price and a loss, and the two are one fact seen from two sides: the more
+/// often the store waits, the less a machine death can take and the less work the store gets done.
+/// Each variant's own documentation owes both sentences, with the number this machine measured —
+/// that documentation is what the crash-point list is written against and what an operator reads.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub enum Policy {
+    /// Wait inside every append, before answering the client.
+    ///
+    /// Document what this costs — how often the store waits for the device, with the cost of one
+    /// wait measured on this machine — and what it loses, which is the question this variant's
+    /// counterpart below answers.
+    PerRecord,
+
+    /// Wait at most once per `millis`, eliding the flush while the interval has not passed.
+    ///
+    /// Document what this costs (a flush per interval: a duty cycle rather than a per-record tax)
+    /// and what it loses (a window of acknowledged writes, bounded by this number).
+    Interval {
+        /// The most time an acknowledged record may sit unflushed, in milliseconds.
+        millis: u64,
+    },
+
+    /// Never wait; the operating system flushes when it decides to.
+    ///
+    /// Document what this costs (nothing at the write path — which is the trap) and what it loses
+    /// (everything written since a flush nobody in this process asked for).
+    Never,
+}
+
+/// What a policy gives up when the **machine** stops — the half of the decision a table can hold.
+///
+/// The variants answer the question `Policy::losses` has to answer for every policy, in a form a
+/// document and a spec can both compare: nothing, a window that is known, and a window nobody can
+/// bound. The variants are the shape; which policy maps to which is the step's work.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub enum Losses {
+    /// Nothing that was acknowledged. A policy with this answer has already waited for the device,
+    /// so a machine death takes only what no client was told about.
+    Nothing,
+    /// A window of acknowledged writes, bounded by `millis` of them.
+    BoundedBy {
+        /// The interval the policy waits at most, in milliseconds.
+        millis: u64,
+    },
+    /// Everything written since the last flush the operating system made on its own schedule.
+    EverythingSinceTheLastFlush,
+}
+
+impl Policy {
+    /// Every policy this build can be asked for.
+    ///
+    /// The list is the enum's public shape: a policy that exists but is not here cannot be named by a
+    /// document, and a policy that is here owes a unique [`Policy::name`]. A policy this build can be
+    /// asked for and that is missing from this list is the drift the step's spec looks for, in both
+    /// directions — the crash-point list has to name every policy in here, and nothing else.
+    pub const ALL: &'static [Policy] = &[Policy::PerRecord, Policy::Never];
+
+    /// What this build does when nothing says otherwise.
+    ///
+    /// The value below is the tempting answer and not the decision: waiting for the device inside
+    /// every append is safe and is a store that cannot keep up with an ingest, and never waiting is a
+    /// store nobody can defend. Replace it with the policy you can argue for from a measurement, and
+    /// put the argument, the raw command and the numbers in `docs/adr/0001-write-path.md` — the
+    /// step's spec checks that this constant and that page name the same policy.
+    pub const DEFAULT: Policy = Policy::PerRecord;
+
+    /// The kind of policy, as a document or a log line names it.
+    ///
+    /// Stable on purpose: the crash-point table, the decision record and a reviewer's `grep` all
+    /// spell a policy this way, so the string is part of the API rather than a display choice. The
+    /// spec reads these strings out of the two documents by them.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn name(&self) -> &'static str {
+        todo!("step lakelet/4.3-write-path-adr")
+    }
+
+    /// The whole policy, including the number an interval carries.
+    ///
+    /// The spelling a document uses when the *number* matters (an interval carries its `millis` in
+    /// it; the other two answers are spelled exactly as [`Policy::name`] spells them), and the
+    /// spelling [`Policy::parse`] has to read back.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn describe(&self) -> String {
+        todo!("step lakelet/4.3-write-path-adr")
+    }
+
+    /// Read a policy back from [`Policy::describe`]'s spelling.
+    ///
+    /// # Errors
+    ///
+    /// [`Error::Invalid`] when `text` is not a policy this build knows, with a sentence naming the
+    /// ones it does: a store that quietly falls back to a default on a typo is a store whose
+    /// durability cannot be read off its configuration, and `Error::Invalid` is what tells a caller
+    /// this was the value's fault rather than the store's.
+    pub fn parse(text: &str) -> Result<Self, Error> {
+        let _ = text;
+        todo!("step lakelet/4.3-write-path-adr")
+    }
+
+    /// What this policy gives up when the machine stops.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn losses(&self) -> Losses {
+        todo!("step lakelet/4.3-write-path-adr")
+    }
+
+    /// `true` when an append has waited for the device before it answered.
+    ///
+    /// The flag a caller branches on, and the other view of [`Policy::losses`]: a policy that loses
+    /// acknowledged records is a policy that did not wait for them.
+    #[must_use]
